    "pageData": {
      "backCoverText": "Every afternoon since her mother entered the Gesundhei nursing home, journalist Lisie Badikhi settles into the spacious ground-floor lounge, only half attentive to the routines of convalescence, when news arrives that the institution's head nurse, Judie Bismuth, has been found dead in her second-floor room. There is no doubt that it is murder, and the circle of suspects quickly closes around the few people present at the time: the director, two staff members, the last three residents still under care that late June, and their relatives who have come to visit. Before long, the victim emerges as a feared woman, one many people had reason to resent.",
      "pressSectionTitle": "Press review extracts",
      "pressColumns": {
        "source": "Press: publication, author, date",
        "extracts": "Extracts"
      },
      "pressRows": [
        {
          "sourceTitle": "Libération",
          "sourceDate": "September 18, 1997",
          "excerpt": "On the day Lisie Badikhi visits her mother at the Gesundhei nursing home in Beersheva, the body of the head nurse is discovered. Lisie, who is a journalist, investigates. She learns that the dead woman had been blackmailing some of the elderly residents, and one by one the masks begin to fall."
        },
        {
          "sourceTitle": "Actualité juive",
          "sourceDate": "May 22, 1997",
          "excerpt": "Fans of Lisie Bidikhi's investigations will find in The Jewel the young journalist untangling the threads of a mysterious double murder. As in her earlier novels, Shulamit Lapid sets her story in Beersheva. This time, social observation and detective suspense meet head-on."
        }
      ],
